On 2007-11-09, sjdevnull@yahoo.com <sjdevnull@yahoo.com> wrote:
> On Nov 9, 9:50 am, Derek Ray <de...@moot.its.only.a.spamtrap.org>
> That seems to me to be good evidence that the game is well-balanced--
> being solved is different from being balanced.

I think it's evidence that the game is _consistent_, which is quite
different from being balanced indeed.

While consistency is a good thing to be in a game, it's also not the
sole metric of a game's balance.

> It's not meant to be a game of random chance. 

"The poisoned orcish arrow hits!  The poison was deadly..."

There's a pretty big subset of "random chance" in there.  While it was
obviously intended in some areas to have random bad luck be an uncommon
occurrence, there's no such limitation on "random positive luck"; the
player can literally trip over a won game.  Even Janis (I think it was
Janis, anyway) says he tends to ignore fully-stocked bones of players
who YASD'd to a cockatrice or stuff, as part of his own effort to keep
it entertaining for himself.

> If even the top players
> couldn't win consistently, then that would be solid evidence that it
> wasn't a well-balanced game IMO, yet you seem to be arguing the
> opposite (which I find confusing).

If the best players aren't winning consistently, it doesn't necessarily 
mean it's not a well-balanced game; perhaps the level of "best" is not
yet high enough to reach that degree of consistency.  

Nethack was this way for many years, and then a mini-explosion of 
improved tools and a subgroup of skilled players all focusing their 
efforts on the game pushed the "best" level up past "solved".

> It's that the early game has
> more diversity and requires more thinking; the post-Castle game, even
> at <200 hp and -17 AC is basically a cakewalk.  I think spork's doing
> a lot to adjust that.

I'm actually doing a lot in the early game as well, but I have to be
careful not to blow up the overall difficulty level -- Nethack is hard
enough for an inexperienced player without adding in random screwjobs
that you have to Know the Magic Tactic or Die.

So some things get easier in the early game, as other changes get made
that possibly make the early game a bit harder.  The gnomish racial
armor really helped out gnomes overall, for example; it's possible that
the protection racket is a more consistent strategy now.  But other
changes have made the racket less of an advantage.

> Maybe it's a problem of amphiboly; the game is balanced quite well in
> terms of how much skill it takes to win consistently, IMO.  It's not
> balanced as well in the distribution within the game of that skill.

This might be a better way to describe the problems.

